  "id": "2026-09-23-history-post-implementation-api-cut-correction",
  "scope": "history",
  "kind": "review",
  "date": "2026-09-23",
  "question": "Does the post-implementation hard cut remain correct after accounting for annotation cloning and replay identity semantics?",
  "model": "GPT-6 / Codex",
  "method": "best-api-review/history-v3; redesign-from-first-principles; corrective identity-transport audit",
  "trigger": "The final consistency pass found that the proposed private replay-claim annotation cut conflicts with the transaction annotation clone boundary.",
  "requirements": [
    "Retain the implemented replay lifecycle and the bounded public session-effect authoring cut from the prior review.",
    "Preserve reference identity for history activation, claimed branch identity and redo insertion anchor across the synchronous claim update.",
    "Do not replace one small private handoff map with scalar identity infrastructure or a clone exception unless another current job requires that machinery.",
    "Keep invalid session-effect configurations impossible in TypeScript through one discriminated public history value."
  ],
  "evidenceReuse": "Reused the preceding post-implementation review and its focused invalid-option TypeScript probe. Freshly traced tx.annotations.set through cloneValue to structuredClone, then traced activation, identity and undoAnchor through beginHistoryReplay and settlement where strict reference equality is required.",
  "summary": "Pursue only the bounded public session-effect authoring cut. Retain the private request-to-claim map: update annotations structured-clone their values, while HistoryReplayClaim carries three opaque reference-identity tokens that later use strict equality. Putting the full claim on the annotation would clone those tokens and break replay attachment and settlement. Replacing them with scalar IDs would add more identity machinery than the existing synchronous private bridge removes. The public defect remains: history: \"session\" and sibling historyReplay still admit runtime-invalid states and should collapse into one discriminated history value.",
  "verdict": "pursue",
  "previous": "2026-09-23-history-post-implementation-api-cut",
  "relation": "supersedes",
  "reconciliation": [
    {
      "record": "2026-09-23-history-post-implementation-api-cut",
      "question": "After implementing the replay lifecycle, should its public and internal ownership be kept as-is, or does one more hard-cut iteration have material value?",
      "action": "supersedes",
      "reason": "Retains the replay-lifecycle verdict and public defineEffect cut, but retracts the private replay-claim map deletion because transaction annotations structured-clone values and the claim depends on opaque reference identities."
    }
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    "Pass the full HistoryReplayClaim through historyReplayClaim: rejected because tx.annotations.set applies structuredClone, producing different activation, branch identity and undo-anchor objects before strict-equality checks.",
    "Replace opaque history identities with scalar IDs so the annotation can carry the claim: rejected because it adds durable identity allocation and propagation solely to remove one synchronous private map.",
    "Add a non-cloning annotation mode: rejected because it weakens the general detached-value invariant for one private handoff and creates a broader public or internal concept without another job.",
    "Keep the request annotation plus private request-to-claim map: strongest internal target. The map preserves reference identity, is bounded to one synchronous update and is deleted in finally.",
    "Keep the public session effect shape unchanged: rejected for the prior probe-proved reason that two invalid configurations compile and fail only at runtime.",
    "Collapse session replay into one discriminated history value: strongest public target; retain the result type when extracted callback authoring needs it, and remove the standalone policy export if no external authoring job remains."
  ],
  "proofLimits": "Corrective source review only. The strict-identity conflict is directly established by structuredClone in cloneValue and === comparisons in history-state; no product code or runtime behavior changed. The existing TypeScript probe still proves the public authoring defect. The proposed nested history contract remains a design target and has not been type-prototyped or implemented.",
